{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-atomic-valence-open-sites","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Definition","doco:Section"],"label":"def:atomic-valence-open-sites","kind":"definition","title":"Valence and lone pairs as open bonding sites","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-electron-shell-braid-capacity"],"tags":["valence","lone-pair","open-sites","covalent-bond"],"leanRef":"qou:QOU.Molecular.atomicValence","text":"atomic-valence-open-sites.md","leanSource":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/molecular-construction/atomic-valence-open-sites.lean","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"def:atomic-valence-open-sites\"></a><p><strong>Definition.</strong>\n<em>The</em> <strong>valence</strong> <em>of an atom (the number of open bonding sites available for covalent bonds) is</em></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">  v(Z) = \\min\\bigl(Z_{\\mathrm{val}},\\; 2n_{\\mathrm{val}}^2 - Z_{\\mathrm{val}}\\bigr)</code></pre>\n<p><em>where <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">Z_{\\mathrm{val}}</code> is the number of valence electrons and <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">n_{\\mathrm{val}}</code> is the outermost shell level.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"left\">Atom</th>\n<th align=\"left\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">Z</code></th>\n<th align=\"left\">Config</th>\n<th align=\"left\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">v(Z)</code></th>\n<th align=\"left\">Open sites</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">H</td>\n<td align=\"left\">1</td>\n<td align=\"left\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1s^1</code></td>\n<td align=\"left\">1</td>\n<td align=\"left\">1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">C</td>\n<td align=\"left\">6</td>\n<td align=\"left\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">[He]\\,2s^2 2p^2</code></td>\n<td align=\"left\">4</td>\n<td align=\"left\">4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">N</td>\n<td align=\"left\">7</td>\n<td align=\"left\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">[He]\\,2s^2 2p^3</code></td>\n<td align=\"left\">3</td>\n<td align=\"left\">3 (+1 lone pair)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">O</td>\n<td align=\"left\">8</td>\n<td align=\"left\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">[He]\\,2s^2 2p^4</code></td>\n<td align=\"left\">2</td>\n<td align=\"left\">2 (+2 lone pairs)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>A</em> <strong>lone pair</strong> <em>is an electron pair whose <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\Phi_1</code> contribution is lower when bound to a single nucleus than when shared. The LP dual assigns electrons to bonding versus lone-pair positions by comparing the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\Phi_1</code> cost of sharing versus retaining.</em></p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/molecular-construction/atomic-valence-open-sites.md","leanStatus":"proved","heading":"Definition","number":"17.3.2"}
